----\n### 3. Non‑sunny diagonal lines\n\nFor any integer \\(s\\) define  \n\n\\[\nd_s:\\; x+y=s . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(d_s\\) is \\(-1\\), so \\(d_s\\) is not sunny.  By definition,\n\\(d_s\\cap S = S_s\\).  Distinct values of \\(s\\) give distinct lines because the\nintercepts differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n2\\le t_1<t_2<\\dots <t_k\\le n+1 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny part.*  Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}=\\{\\ell_{t_1},\\ell_{t_2},\\dots ,\\ell_{t_k}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{2}\\) is given by (8) and for \\(t\\ge3\\) by (6).  By (7)–(8) each\n\\(\\ell_{t_i}\\) contains exactly the anti‑diagonal \\(S_{t_i}\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  For every remaining sum  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\n\\]\n\nadd the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) from (9).  There are \\(n-k\\) such sums, so we add\nexactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nThus the total family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}\\;\\cup\\;\\{d_s\\mid s\\text{ as above}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Verification of the two required conditions\n\n*Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and set \\(s=a+b\\).  \nIf \\(s=t_i\\) for some \\(i\\), then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{t_i}\\) by (7)–(8).  \nIf \\(s\\) is not among the chosen \\(t_i\\), then \\((a,b)\\in d_s\\) by (9).  \nHence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  By construction \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{t_i}\\), all of which are sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\)\nlines are the non‑sunny diagonals \\(d_s\\).  Therefore the family has precisely\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---------------------------------