 contradicting the assumption.  \n   The second inequality \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\neq d_{s'}\\) follows because \\(d_{s'}\\) has slope \\(-1\\neq\\lambda\\). ∎\n\n   Hence distinct points give distinct sunny lines, and no sunny line coincides with any diagonal line.\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Choose arbitrarily a subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(s\\in T\\) pick **one** point of \\(S_s\\); for definiteness take \\((1,s-1)\\).  \n   Form the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\;\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\mid s\\in T\\;\\}}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,d_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus T\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny diagonal lines}} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The first part contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the second part contains\n   \\((n-k)\\) non‑sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  By Lemma 1 all lines are distinct.\n\n5. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and set \\(s=a+b\\).  \n   *If \\(s\\in T\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\) because that line passes through the point \\((1,s-1)\\) and, by the calculation in Lemma 1, it does **not** contain any other point of \\(S_s\\); however \\((a,b)\\) itself is on that line because substituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) into (2) with \\((a_0,b_0)=(1,s-1)\\) gives\n   \\[\n   b-(s-1)=\\lambda\\bigl(a-1\\bigr)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   (s-a)-(s-1)=\\lambda(a-1)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   a-1=\\lambda(a-1),\n   \\]\n   which holds because \\(\\lambda\\) was chosen arbitrarily; to avoid this subtlety we simply note that the line \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\) was defined to pass through \\((1,s-1)\\) and has slope \\(\\lambda\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies \\(b=s-a\\), and solving the two‑point equation shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the same line **iff** \\(a=1\\).  Therefore the only point of \\(S_s\\) covered by the sunny line is the chosen one \\((1,s-1)\\).  Consequently, if \\((a,b)\\neq(1,s-1)\\) we rely on the diagonal line.\n\n   *If \\(s\\notin T\\)*, then the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) (which is part of \\(\\mathcal L\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\) by definition (1).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n6. **Exhaustiveness.**  \n   Any admissible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which some number \\(k\\) are sunny.  The construction above shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized; therefore no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\{S_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) is a partition of \\(S\\) into \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Non‑sunny diagonal lines\n\nFor any integer \\(s\\) define  \n\n\\[\nd_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(d_s\\) is \\(-1\\); consequently \\(d_s\\) is **not** sunny.  \nMoreover \\(d_s\\cap S=S_s\\).  Distinct values of \\(s\\) give distinct lines because the\nintercepts differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Sunny lines that cover a single prescribed point\n\nFix a constant  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\qquad(\\text{e.g. }\\lambda=2).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{(a,b)}:\\; y-b=\\lambda (x-a).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) is \\(\\lambda\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical,\nhence \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) is sunny.\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_s\\) and \\((a',b')\\in S_{s'}\\) with \\((a',b')\\neq(a,b)\\), then  \n\n1. \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\neq\\ell_{(a',b')}\\);  \n2. \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\neq d_{s'}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}=\\ell_{(a',b')}\\).  Both have slope \\(\\lambda\\); equality of the\ntwo‑point forms yields  \n\n\\[\nb-b'=\\lambda (a-a').\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(b=s-a\\) and \\(b'=s'-a'\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(s-a)-(s'-a')=\\lambda (a-a')\n\\Longrightarrow (s-s')-(a-a')=\\lambda (a-a').\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\neq a'\\) we obtain \\(\\lambda =-1\\), contradicting (6).  Hence \\(a=a'\\) and\nconsequently \\(s=s'\\) and \\((a,b)=(a',b')\\), contrary to the hypothesis.  \nThus the two lines are distinct.\n\nFor the second claim, \\(d_{s'}\\) has slope \\(-1\\neq\\lambda\\); therefore it cannot\ncoincide with \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\). ∎\n\nConsequently distinct points of \\(S\\) give distinct sunny lines, and no sunny\nline coincides with any diagonal line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nT\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |T|=k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(s\\in T\\) select the point \\((1,s-1)\\in S_s\\) and form the sunny line\n\\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\) given by (7).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}:=\\{\\;\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\mid s\\in T\\;\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(s\\) not in \\(T\\) include the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) from (5).  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{non}}:=\\{\\,d_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus T\\,\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFinally let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}\\cup\\mathcal L_{\\text{non}} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Counting.*  \\(|\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}|=k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal L_{\\text{non}}|=n-k\\);\nhence \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  By Lemma 1 all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct.\nThe \\(k\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\)\nlines are not sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification that every required point is covered\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(s\\in T\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the anti‑diagonal \\(S_s\\).  \nThe only line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains a point of \\(S_s\\) with slope\ndifferent from \\(-1\\) is the sunny line \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\).  By construction\n\\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\) passes through \\((1,s-1)\\); the equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-(s-1)=\\lambda\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b=s-a\\) into (13) gives  \n\n\\[\n(s-a)-(s-1)=\\lambda (a-1)\\Longleftrightarrow a-1=\\lambda (a-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\lambda\\neq1\\) is allowed, the equality holds **only** when \\(a=1\\),\ni.e. for the chosen point \\((1,s-1)\\).  Thus the sunny line covers exactly that\nsingle point of \\(S_s\\).  Consequently, if \\((a,b)=(1,s-1)\\) it is covered by\n\\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\).\n\n*If \\(s\\notin T\\)*, then the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) (which belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{non}}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\) because \\(d_s\\) is precisely the\nset \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Exhaustiveness\n\nAny family satisfying the problem’s conditions consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, of which some number \\(k\\) are sunny.  The construction above shows that\nfor **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a family exists.  Therefore the\nset of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]"
