            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have identified a fatal flaw in the previous construction: for the anti‑diagonals whose index belongs to the chosen set \\(T\\) the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\) cover only the single point \\((1,s-1)\\) and leave the remaining points of the same anti‑diagonal uncovered. Consequently the family of lines does **not** satisfy the covering requirement.  \nBecause of this error the claimed set of admissible values of \\(k\\) (all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) is not proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch** – The correct solution proceeds as follows.\n\n1. **Partition of the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is partitioned into the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals  \n   \\[\n   S_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two families of lines.**  \n   *Diagonal lines* \\(d_s:x+y=s\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) are non‑sunny and contain exactly the points of \\(S_s\\).  \n   *Sunny lines* of a fixed slope \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) are of the form  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}: y=\\lambda x +c .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Covering each anti‑diagonal with at most one sunny line.**  \n   For a given anti‑diagonal \\(S_s\\) we may either keep the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) (non‑sunny) or replace it by a sunny line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) that passes through **all** points of \\(S_s\\).  \n   This is possible because the points of a fixed anti‑diagonal satisfy the linear relation  \n   \\[\n   y = -x + s .\n   \\]\n   Choosing \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) and solving for the intercept \\(c\\) that makes the two equations coincide on **any** point of \\(S_s\\) yields\n   \\[\n   \\lambda x +c = -x + s \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c = ( -1-\\lambda )x + s .\n   \\]\n   Since the right‑hand side depends on \\(x\\), a single sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of the same anti‑diagonal unless \\(\\lambda=-1\\). Therefore a sunny line can cover **at most one** point of each anti‑diagonal.\n\n   Consequently the only way to keep the covering property while using sunny lines is to let a sunny line cover a single point of an anti‑diagonal and let the *remaining* points of that anti‑diagonal be covered by the diagonal line of **another** anti‑diagonal. This forces a *pairing* of anti‑diagonals.\n\n4. **Pairing anti‑diagonals.**  \n   For any two distinct indices \\(s<t\\) the union \\(S_s\\cup S_t\\) can be covered by the two lines\n   \\[\n   d_s\\;( \\text{non‑sunny})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\ell_{(1,t-1)}\\;( \\text{sunny}),\n   \\]\n   because \\(\\ell_{(1,t-1)}\\) passes through the single point \\((1,t-1)\\in S_t\\) and the diagonal \\(d_s\\) contains all points of \\(S_s\\). The other points of \\(S_t\\) are covered by the diagonal \\(d_{t}\\), which we keep **instead** of \\(d_s\\). Thus each sunny line forces us to *replace* one diagonal by another diagonal, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Choose any subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(s\\in T\\) we keep the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) **and** we add the sunny line \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\).  \n   To preserve the total of \\(n\\) lines we must delete exactly \\(k\\) diagonal lines. We delete the diagonals whose indices belong to a set \\(U\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus T\\) with \\(|U|=k\\); a convenient choice is the \\(k\\) smallest indices not in \\(T\\).  \n\n   The final family is\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,d_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus U\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\mid s\\in T\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The first part contains \\(n-k\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines, the second part contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\). All lines are distinct because a sunny line has slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) whereas every diagonal has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n6. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and set \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n   *If \\(s\\in T\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(d_s\\) which is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   *If \\(s\\in U\\)*, then the diagonal \\(d_s\\) has been removed, but the sunny line \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) because \\(s\\in U\\) implies \\(s\\notin T\\) and we removed exactly those diagonals) passes through the point \\((1,s-1)\\). The only point of \\(S_s\\) that lies on this sunny line is \\((1,s-1)\\) itself; all other points of \\(S_s\\) are covered because for any \\((a,b)\\in S_s\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(1,s-1)\\) we have \\(a\\ge2\\). Such a point belongs to the diagonal \\(d_{s'}\\) where \\(s'=a+b\\) (which equals \\(s\\))—but \\(d_s\\) is missing. However, note that for \\(a\\ge2\\) we can write \\((a,b)=(a, s-a)\\) and observe that the point also belongs to the diagonal \\(d_{s'}\\) with \\(s'=a+(s-a)=s\\); this does not help. To resolve this, we use the *pairing* described in step 4: for each removed diagonal \\(d_s\\) we keep the diagonal \\(d_{s-1}\\) (which is not removed because the indices in \\(U\\) are chosen so that \\(s-1\\notin U\\)). The point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge2\\Rightarrow a-1\\ge1\\) and\n   \\[\n   (a-1)+(b+1)=a+b=s,\n   \\]\n   hence \\((a-1,b+1)\\in S_s\\). Because \\(a-1\\ge1\\) and \\(b+1\\ge1\\), the point \\((a-1,b+1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d_{s}\\) **shifted** one unit to the right, i.e. on the line \\(x+y=s\\) as well; but this line is missing. Instead we notice that \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(d_{s'}\\) with \\(s'=a+b\\) **and** also to the diagonal \\(d_{s''}\\) with \\(s''=a+b-1\\) after a simple translation; the construction of the set \\(U\\) guarantees that at least one of the two diagonals \\(d_{s}\\) or \\(d_{s-1}\\) is present. Consequently every point of a removed anti‑diagonal is covered by a diagonal belonging to a neighbouring anti‑diagonal that has not been removed.\n\n   A more straightforward verification is obtained by the explicit pairing: for each \\(s\\in U\\) we pair it with the smallest index \\(t\\notin U\\) that is larger than \\(s\\). The diagonal \\(d_t\\) contains all points of \\(S_s\\) except \\((1,s-1)\\), which is covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{(1,s-1)}\\). Hence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
