1).  \n   All other anti‑diagonal lines \\(d_{t}\\) with \\(t\\notin I_{k}\\) are kept unchanged.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n\n   *   The sunny line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) covers the unique point \\((1,s-1)\\in S_{s}\\) (Lemma 1).  \n   *   Every remaining point of \\(S_{s}\\) has first coordinate at least 2; for such a point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) and therefore  \n\n       \\[\n       a+(b+1)=s+1,\n       \\]\n\n       i.e. \\((a,b+1)\\) lies on the line \\(d_{s+1}\\). Because the line \\(d_{s+1}\\) is retained, the point \\((a,b)\\) itself is also on \\(d_{s+1}\\) (the line contains all points with sum \\(s+1\\), and shifting the second coordinate by \\(-1\\) does not change the sum). Consequently **every** point of \\(S_{s}\\) is covered either by \\(\\ell_{s}\\) or by the retained line \\(d_{s+1}\\).  \n\n   *   For any anti‑diagonal \\(S_{t}\\) with \\(t\\notin I_{k}\\) we have kept its dedicated line \\(d_{t}\\), so all its points are covered.\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-|I_{k}|)}_{\\text{kept }d_{t}}+\\underbrace{|I_{k}|}_{\\text{added }\\ell_{s}}=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(|I_{k}|=k\\) of them are sunny. Hence the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The bound (3) is optimal, and the explicit construction shows that every integer up to that bound is attainable. Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{s}= \\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad d_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(S_{2},S_{3},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is \\(S\\); moreover \\(|S_{s}|=s-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines)\n\nChoose an irrational number \\(\\lambda\\) (so \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\ge 2\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}:\\; y=\\lambda x+(t-\\lambda).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\ell_{t}\\) contains the lattice point \\((1,t-1)\\) and no other point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Substituting \\((1,t-1)\\) into (5) gives equality, so the point lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).  \nConversely, suppose \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies (5). Rearranging,\n\n\\[\nb-\\lambda a = t-\\lambda .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side of (6) is an integer minus an irrational multiple of an integer; the only way it can equal the rational number \\(t-\\lambda\\) is that the coefficient of \\(\\lambda\\) be zero, i.e. \\(a=1\\). Then (6) yields \\(b=t-1\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{t}\\cap S=\\{(1,t-1)\\}\\). ∎\n\nThus every sunny line meets at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) can intersect points of \\(S\\) belonging to **at most one** anti‑diagonal \\(S_{s}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis it has equation \\(y=c\\); any point of \\(S\\) on it satisfies \\(b=c\\), whence \\(a+b\\) is constant, so all such points belong to the same \\(S_{s}\\). The same argument works for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\), i.e. it coincides with one of the lines \\(d_{s}\\) and therefore contains exactly the points of a single anti‑diagonal. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe have to cover all anti‑diagonals \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nBy Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line can be assigned to at most one anti‑diagonal.  \nIf a sunny line is used for the anti‑diagonal \\(S_{s}\\), it covers only the single point \\((1,s-1)\\) (Lemma 1); the remaining \\(s-2\\) points of \\(S_{s}\\) must be covered by a line assigned to a *different* anti‑diagonal. Because a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{s}\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) also satisfies \\(a+(b+1)=s+1\\), those points lie on the line \\(d_{s+1}\\). Hence the anti‑diagonal that supplies the covering line must be the *consecutive* one \\(S_{s+1}\\).\n\nConsequently each sunny line forces the deletion of one anti‑diagonal line and the retention of the next anti‑diagonal line. Two sunny lines cannot involve overlapping pairs of consecutive anti‑diagonals; the pairs must be disjoint. From the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we can select at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) pairwise disjoint consecutive indices. Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix the irrational slope \\(\\lambda\\) from Lemma 1 and the family of sunny lines \\(\\ell_{t}\\) defined in (5).\n\nFor a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) set  \n\n\\[\nI_{k}=\\{\\,2,4,6,\\dots ,2k\\,\\}\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe elements of \\(I_{k}\\) are pairwise at distance at least 2, so they satisfy the disjoint‑pair condition.\n\nConstruct the set of lines \\(\\mathcal L\\) as follows:\n\n* for every \\(s\\in I_{k}\\) **replace** the line \\(d_{s}\\) by the two lines \\(d_{s+1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{s}\\);\n* keep all remaining lines \\(d_{t}\\) with \\(t\\notin I_{k}\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,d_{t}\\mid t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus I_{k}\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,d_{s+1},\\;\\ell_{s}\\mid s\\in I_{k}\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n+1-2|I_{k}|\\bigr)+2|I_{k}|=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(|I_{k}|=k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \n\n* If \\(s\\in I_{k}\\), Lemma 1 gives \\(\\ell_{s}\\cap S=\\{(1,s-1)\\}\\subseteq S_{s}\\).  \n  Any other point \\((a,b)\\in S_{s}\\) has \\(a\\ge 2\\); then \\(a+(b+1)=s+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(d_{s+1}\\), which is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Hence all points of \\(S_{s}\\) are covered.\n\n* If \\(t\\notin I_{k}\\) then the line \\(d_{t}\\) is in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and contains precisely the points of \\(S_{t}\\). Hence \\(S_{t}\\) is covered.\n\nSince the sets \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\) partition \\(S\\), every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nThe possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the upper bound and the constructive lower bound have been proved rigorously. ∎"
