t most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) can intersect points of \\(S\\) belonging to **at most one** anti‑diagonal \\(S_{s}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis it has equation \\(y=c\\); any point of \\(S\\) on it satisfies \\(b=c\\), whence \\(a+b\\) is constant, so all such points belong to the same \\(S_{s}\\). The same argument works for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\), i.e. it coincides with one of the lines \\(d_{s}\\) and therefore contains exactly the points of a single anti‑diagonal. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe have to cover all anti‑diagonals \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nBy Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line can be assigned to at most one anti‑diagonal.  \nIf a sunny line is used for the anti‑diagonal \\(S_{s}\\), it covers only the single point \\((1,s-1)\\) (Lemma 1); the remaining \\(s-2\\) points of \\(S_{s}\\) must be covered by a line assigned to a *different* anti‑diagonal. Because a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{s}\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) also satisfies \\(a+(b+1)=s+1\\), those points lie on the line \\(d_{s+1}\\). Hence the anti‑diagonal that supplies the covering line must be the *consecutive* one \\(S_{s+1}\\).\n\nConsequently each sunny line forces the deletion of one anti‑diagonal line and the retention of the next anti‑diagonal line. Two sunny lines cannot involve overlapping pairs of consecutive anti‑diagonals; the pairs must be disjoint. From the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we can select at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) pairwise disjoint consecutive indices. Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n-------------------------------------