n\\) is still covered.  \n\n   Because the construction works for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and diagonal \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nhence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nAll \\(L_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(n\\) distinct lines and \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A basic replacement lemma  \n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). There exists a sunny line that contains the point \\((i,1)\\) and also a point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on the vertical line \\(x=i+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,1)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \nTheir connecting line has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{1-1}{i+1-i}=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is horizontal and therefore **not** sunny.  \nInstead choose \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\) (the latter belongs to \\(T_n\\) because \\((i+1)+2\\le n+1\\) for all admissible \\(i\\)).  \nThe slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{2-1}{1}=1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1,\n\\]\n\nso the line through these two points is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line  \n\nFix an index \\(i\\) and delete the vertical line \\(x=i\\) from the covering of §1.  \nAdd the sunny line supplied by Lemma 1; denote it by \\(S_i\\).  \n\n*Coverage of points formerly on \\(x=i\\).*  \n\n* The point \\((i,1)\\) now lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n* Any other point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) belongs to the diagonal line  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{i+b}:\\;x+y=i+b,\n  \\]\n\n  which is one of the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n  All these diagonal lines are still present (they have never been removed), so every \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) remains covered.\n\nThus after the replacement the set of lines still covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe new line \\(S_i\\) is sunny, while the remaining vertical lines are unsunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nSelect any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) perform the replacement described in §3, obtaining the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \nThe final family of lines consists of  \n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\), and  \n* the \\(n-k\\) untouched vertical lines \\(\\{x=j\\mid j\\notin I\\}\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because each \\(S_i\\) contains the point \\((i,1)\\) which does not lie on any other line of the family.  \nBy the argument of §3, every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.  \nHence we have constructed, for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all required points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of other values  \n\nThe total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\); consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.  \nThe constructions above show that every integer in this interval is attainable.  \nTherefore no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
