ly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nIf we replace a vertical line \\(L_i\\) by a sunny line \\(S\\), the line \\(S\\) can intersect the column \\(x=i\\) in at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise \\(S\\) would be vertical).  Hence all other points of the set  \n\n\\[\nP_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.  Since each of those remaining lines is either vertical, horizontal or diagonal (the only three directions that can pass through more than one point of a given column), and the horizontal and diagonal directions are also forbidden for being sunny, any line that is not vertical can again meet the column \\(x=i\\) in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently, after the removal of \\(L_i\\) we would need at least \\(|P_i|-1\\) additional lines to cover the points of \\(P_i\\) that are lost, which is impossible when the total number of lines is constrained to be \\(n\\).\n\nThis observation shows a serious obstruction to increasing the number of sunny lines, but it does **not** constitute a complete impossibility proof, because one could in principle redesign the whole set of \\(n\\) lines (rather than starting from the vertical covering) so that the required coverage is achieved with several sunny lines simultaneously.  I was unable to construct such a redesign, nor to prove that it cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\n- For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that contains **no** sunny lines; i.e. \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n- The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\) sunny lines remains an open question.  \n\nThus the problem is partially solved: the admissible value \\(k=0\\) is confirmed, while the full classification of admissible \\(k\\) is not yet established.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
