            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{L_i : x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nso every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_i\\) with the same first\ncoordinate.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(T_n\\) and containing \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is admissible for\nevery \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma: a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one point  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{If a line }\\ell\\text{ is sunny, then }|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in parametric form\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+t(p,q),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R,\n\\]\nwhere \\((p,q)\\) is a primitive integer direction vector, i.e. \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), and \\((p,q)\\neq(0,0)\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis we have \\(q\\neq0\\);  \nbecause it is **not** parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis we have \\(p\\neq0\\);  \nbecause it is **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\).\n\nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThen there exist integers \\(t_1<t_2\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n(x_i,y_i)=(x_0,y_0)+t_i(p,q)\\in T_n\\qquad(i=1,2).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two expressions gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_2-x_1,y_2-y_1)=(t_2-t_1)(p,q) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the coordinates of the points are integers, \\(t_2-t_1\\) must be an\ninteger; denote \\(m:=t_2-t_1\\ge1\\).  Hence the vector joining the two points is\n\\((mp,mq)\\).\n\nBoth points satisfy the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\):\n\\[\nx_i+y_i\\le n+1\\qquad(i=1,2).\n\\]\nSubtracting these inequalities yields\n\\[\n(mp+mq)=m(p+q)\\le 0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m>0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)),\n(3) forces \\(p+q<0\\).\n\nNow consider the point \\((x_1,y_1)\\).  Since it belongs to \\(T_n\\) we have\n\\(x_1\\ge1,\\;y_1\\ge1\\).  Adding the vector \\((p,q)\\) once (i.e. moving from\n\\((x_1,y_1)\\) to \\((x_1+p,y_1+q)\\)) we obtain another lattice point on the same\nline.  Because \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\), at least one of the coordinates\ndecreases (recall \\(p+q<0\\)).  Consequently either \\(x_1+p\\le0\\) or\n\\(y_1+q\\le0\\); in either case the new point lies **outside** the region\n\\(a,b>0\\).  Hence the only lattice points of the line that can stay inside the\nfirst quadrant are those with parameter \\(t\\) belonging to a half‑open interval\nof length strictly less than \\(1\\).  Therefore the line cannot contain two\ndistinct points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus any sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of a covering containing a sunny line  \n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the lemma each sunny line contributes at most one covered point, so the\nsunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are not sunny; each of them is parallel to one of\nthe three forbidden directions.\n\n* If a line is vertical, say \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), then the points of\n  \\(T_n\\) on it are \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); at most \\(n\\) points.\n* If a line is horizontal, \\(y=j\\), the same bound holds.\n* If a line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\), then\n  the intersection with \\(T_n\\) consists of the points \\((a,c-a)\\) where\n  \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); the maximal size is \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\le n\\).\n\nHence **every** non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by\n\\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F} (L\\cap T_n)|\n\\le k\\cdot1+(n-k)\\cdot n\n= n^{2}-k(n-1)\n\\le n^{2}-(n-1)=n^{2}-n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+1 \\;<\\; \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause the inequality is equivalent to \\(n^{2}-3n+2<0\\), i.e. \\((n-1)(n-2)<0\\),\nwhich holds precisely for \\(n=2\\); for \\(n\\ge3\\) the left‑hand side is\nstrictly smaller.  Therefore (4) cannot reach the required cardinality\n\\(|T_n|\\) when \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\nThus a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines **cannot** contain any sunny\nline.  The only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\