nny line  \n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nA line \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points\n\\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the number of such points is\n\\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nSimilarly, a line \\(y=c\\) meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  Intersecting with the\nconditions \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) yields the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1,\\;c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the number of points is \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,n+1-c\\}\\).  The maximum of this\nexpression over integer \\(c\\) occurs at \\(c=\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\rfloor\\) and\nequals \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\), which is certainly \\(\\le n\\).\n\nThus **every** non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of a covering containing a sunny line  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  \n\n* By the Lemma, each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\); the\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.\n\n* The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, and each of them covers at most\n  \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Section 3).  \n\nTherefore the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot1+(n-k)\\cdot n=n^{2}-k(n-1)\\le n^{2}-n+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\).  For \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+1<\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nbecause (10) is equivalent to \\(n^{2}-3n+2<0\\), i.e. \\((n-1)(n-2)<0\\), which\nholds precisely for \\(n=2\\); for all \\(n\\ge3\\) the left‑hand side is strictly\nsmaller.  Consequently the bound (9) is insufficient to cover all points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Hence no covering of \\(T