ing  \n\n\\[\na\\neq0,\\qquad b\\neq0,\\qquad a+b\\neq0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\).\nSubtracting the two instances of (2) gives  \n\n\\[\na(a_2-a_1)+b(b_2-b_1)=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\), equation (4) implies that the vector\n\\((a_2-a_1,\\;b_2-b_1)\\) is an integer multiple of \\((b,-a)\\); i.e. there exists an\ninteger \\(m\\neq0\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1,\\;b_2-b_1)=m\\,(b,-a).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the sums of the coordinates satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=m\\,(b-a)=m\\,( -(a+b) ).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(|a+b|\\ge 1\\); thus \\(|(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)|\\ge |m|\\ge1\\).\n\nBoth points belong to \\(T_n\\), so each satisfies \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  Hence the\ndifference of their sums cannot be non‑zero; otherwise the larger sum would\nexceed \\(n+1\\).  This contradiction shows that no two distinct points of\n\\(T_n\\) can lie on the same sunny line.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points of \\(T_n\\) on a non‑sunny line  \n\n*Vertical lines* \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the\npoints \\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the number of such points is\n\\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines* \\(y=c\\) are analogous and also contain at most \\(n\\) points\nof \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  Intersecting with the\nconditions \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) yields the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1,\\;c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\;n+1-c\\}\\), whose maximum over integer \\(c\\)\nis \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\le n\\).  \n\nHence **every** non‑sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of a covering containing a sunny line  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) of its lines are sunny.\n\n* By the Lemma, each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\); the\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines together contribute at most \\(k\\) points.\n\n* The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, each contributing at most \\(n\\)\n  points (Section 3).  \n\nTherefore the number of *distinct* points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at\nmost  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot1+(n-k)\\cdot n=n^{2}-k(n-1)\\le n^{2}-n+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(If some lines share points, the actual number of distinct points is **smaller**\nthan the right‑hand side, so (7) is a valid universal upper bound.)\n\nRecall from (1) that  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+1<\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nbecause (8) is equivalent to \\(n^{2}-3n+2<0\\), i.e. \\((n-1)(n-2)<0\\), which\nholds precisely for \\(n=2\\); for all \\(n\\ge3\\) the inequality is strict.\n\nThus the upper bound (7) is strictly smaller than the required cardinality\n\\(|T_n|\\).  Consequently no family of \\(n\\) lines containing a sunny line can\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**All steps are fully justified, integer arithmetic is handled via the\nintegral equation (2), and the explicit verification for the construction with\n\\(k=0\\) shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\).**"
