ion is performed as follows.  \n   Choose the horizontal line \\(y=r\\) (with \\(1\\le r\\le n\\)) that appears in \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\).  \n   Replace it by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_r:\\; y = -x + (r+1) .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_r\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.  \n   Observe that for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\),\n\n   \\[\n   S_r\\cap\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}= (a,\\;r+1-a),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(S_r\\) contains precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on the diagonal segment from \\((1,r)\\) to \\((r,1)\\).  \n   Those points were previously covered by the horizontal line \\(y=r\\) (which contains \\((a,r)\\) for all \\(a\\)) and by the vertical line \\(x=r\\) (which contains \\((r,b)\\) for all \\(b\\)).  \n   Since both \\(y=r\\) and \\(x=r\\) already belong to the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\), the removal of \\(y=r\\) does not create any uncovered point: each point \\((a,r)\\) with \\(a\\le r\\) is still on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), and each point \\((r,b)\\) with \\(b\\le r\\) is still on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   The only points that could be lost are those with both coordinates \\(\\le r\\) and lying on the diagonal \\(a+b=r+1\\); these are exactly the points now supplied by \\(S_r\\).\n\n   Consequently the new family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\setminus\\{y=r\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_r\\}\n   \\]\n\n   still covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and has one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\).  \n   Repeating this replacement \\(k\\) times (choosing distinct values of \\(r\\) each time) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps 2 and 4 together give the full description of the possible values of \\(k\\): all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\) are attainable, while \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\; \\text{a family }\\mathcal L\\text{ of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }\\mathcal P_n\n\\text{ and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound: \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it meets each vertical line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) in exactly one point.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a given vertical line.\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,\\,n+1-i\\bigr),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n+1-i\\) points.  \nSince each sunny line contributes at most one of them, at least \\(n+1-i\\) distinct sunny lines are needed to cover the points on \\(x=i\\).\n\nSumming over all \\(i\\) gives a lower bound on the total number of incidences (point–line contacts) required:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(n+1-i)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, each of the \\(n\\) sunny lines can meet each of the \\(n\\) vertical lines in at most one point, so the total number of incidences that the \\(n\\) sunny lines can provide is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot n=n^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad\\text{(since }n+1>2n\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThus (1) exceeds (2), a contradiction.  \nHence a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible; any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for all }k\\in\\mathcal K_n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains no sunny line.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n0\\in\\mathcal K_n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case* \\(k=0\\) is given by (4).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBecause \\(k-1\\le n-2\\), \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) contains at least two non‑sunny lines.  \nSelect a horizontal non‑sunny line among them; without loss of generality it is  \n\n\\[\nH_r:\\; y=r\\qquad\\text{for some }r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_r:\\; y=-x+(r+1).\n\\]\n\n\\(S_r\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Replacing \\(H_r\\) by \\(S_r\\) preserves coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on \\(H_r\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on the vertical line \\(x=r\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(r,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-r\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with both coordinates \\(\\le r\\) and satisfying \\(a+b=r+1