            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=0,\\qquad x=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nWe determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\;\\mathcal L\\subset\\{\\text{lines in }\\mathbb R^{2}\\},\n\\;|\\mathcal L|=n,\\;\n\\forall (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\;\\exists L\\in\\mathcal L:\\;(a,b)\\in L,\n\\;\n\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ sunny}\\}=k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nFor every integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) we have \\((1,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(1+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence \\(x=1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nA sunny line is **not** vertical, therefore it meets the line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \nConsequently at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required to cover the \\(n\\) points on \\(x=1\\).  \nSince we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, one of the lines must be vertical, i.e. non‑sunny.  \nThus any admissible family contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nTherefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nHence \\(0\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction of families with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case* \\(k=0\\) is given by the family of horizontal lines of Section 2.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBecause \\(k-1\\le n-2\\), at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) are non‑sunny.  \nAmong the non‑sunny lines choose a horizontal one; denote it by  \n\n\\[\nH_r:\\; y=r\\qquad\\text{for some }r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_r:\\; y=-x+(r+1).\n\\]\n\n\\(S_r\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it is sunny.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\setminus\\{H_r\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_r\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) still covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Points that were on \\(H_r\\).*  \nThese are the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\le r\\) then \\((a,r)=(a,r+1-a)\\) belongs to the diagonal segment  \n\n\\[\nD_r=\\{(a,r+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le r\\},\n\\]\n\nand therefore lies on \\(S_r\\).\n\nIf \\(a>r\\) then \\((a,r)\\) also lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\): the original family for \\(k=0\\) consisted solely of horizontal lines, and during each inductive step we never delete a vertical line (we only delete the chosen horizontal line).  \nHence \\(x=a\\) remains in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\), so \\((a,r)\\) stays covered.\n\n*Points that were on the vertical line \\(x=r\\).*  \nThese are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(r,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-r\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b\\le r\\) then \\((r,b)=(r+1-b,b)\\) belongs to \\(D_r\\) and is now on \\(S_r\\).\n\nIf \\(b>r\\) then \\((r,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which has not been removed (the only horizontal line removed is \\(y=r\\)).  \nThus \\((r,b)\\) remains covered.\n\nAll other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are untouched by the replacement, so they stay covered as well.  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\), and still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nRepeating the replacement for distinct values of \\(r\\) we obtain, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfying the required conditions.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determination of \\(\\mathcal K_n\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\mathcal K_n\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); from (6) we have the reverse inclusion.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn words: for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act