d L\\text{ sunny}\\}=k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nThe vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\in\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, so it meets \\(x=1\\) in at most one of those points.  \nThus at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required to cover the \\(n\\) points on \\(x=1\\).  \nSince we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, at least one of them must be vertical, i.e. non\u2011sunny.  \nHence any admissible family contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).  \nAll \\(V_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(0\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction of families with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Base case \\(k=0\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_0=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) constructed in \u00a72.\n\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) (with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)) exists.  \nBecause \\(k-1\\le n-2\\), at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nAll non\u2011sunny lines in our families are vertical (the construction never creates horizontal or \\(x+y=0\\) lines).  \nHence there is a vertical line in \\(\\cal L_{k-1}\\); choose one, denote it by  \n\n\\[\nV_r:\\;x=r\\qquad (1\\le r\\le n).\n\\]\n\nDefine