            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=0,\\qquad x=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nThe vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\in\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, so it can meet this set in at most one point.  \nThus at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required to cover those \\(n\\) points.  \nSince we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, at least one of them must be vertical, i.e. non‑sunny.  \nConsequently any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=-x+(i+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is a translate of the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions and is sunny.\n\nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_n:\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_{n-1},V_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct (different intercepts for the \\(S_i\\) and different \\(x\\)-coordinates for the vertical line).  \n\\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=1\\); the point lies on \\(V_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) set \\(i=a+b-1\\).  \n  Since \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  \n  Substituting \\(i\\) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  b = -a + (i+1)= -a + (a+b)=b,\n  \\]\n\n  so \\((a,b)\\in S_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing sunny lines by vertical lines\n\nFor any subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_T = \\bigl(\\{S_i\\mid i\\notin T\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\bigl(\\{V_i\\mid i\\in T\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{V_n\\},\n\\qquad\\text{where }V_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n* The lines \\(V_i\\;(i\\in T)\\) and \\(V_n\\) are vertical, hence non‑sunny.\n* The lines \\(S_i\\;(i\\notin T)\\) are sunny.\n* All lines are distinct: different vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, and each sunny line has slope \\(-1\\) and a distinct intercept, so it cannot coincide with a vertical line.\n\nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_T\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus T| = n-1-|T| . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n\\) the point lies on \\(V_n\\), which is always present.\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) set \\(i=a+b-1\\) (as in §2).  \n  - If \\(i\\in T\\) then the vertical line \\(V_i\\) (i.e. \\(x=i\\)) contains the point because its \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(a=i\\).  \n  - If \\(i\\notin T\\) the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Realising every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nGiven an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), choose a subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size  \n\n\\[\n|T| = n-1-k .\n\\]\n\nBy (2) the family \\(\\mathcal L_T\\) then contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (1) shows that \\(k\\) cannot equal \\(n\\).  \nThe constructions of §§2–4 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
