the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\qquad(\\text{so }a+b=i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Through \\(P_i\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-i) \\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)} .\n   \\]\n\n   * The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each\n     \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n   * The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because they have different\n     \\(x\\)-intercepts.\n   * No \\(L_i\\) coincides with any diagonal line \\(D_c\\) (the latter have slope\n     \\(-1\\)).\n\n   Replace, for each \\(i\\), the diagonal line \\(D_{i+1}\\) (the line that originally\n   contained the point \\(P_i\\)) by the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n\n   After the replacement we have the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\bigr\\}\\cup\n                \\bigl\\{D_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The first set contributes exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the second set contributes\n   the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines; altogether there are \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\((a,b)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line\n   \\(L_i\\) by construction.\n\n   *Otherwise* \\((a,b)\\neq P_i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n   Its sum \\(s=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(s\\notin\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) then the diagonal line \\(D_s\\) is still present in\n   \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   If \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) then \\(s=i+1\\) for a unique \\(i\\le k\\).  \n   In this case \\((a,b)\\neq P_i\\) but both points have the same sum \\(s\\); the only\n   point of the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) that was removed is \\(P_i\\).  Hence all other\n   points of \\(D_{i+1}\\), including \\((a,b)\\), remain covered by the unchanged\n   diagonal line \\(D_{i+1}\\) (w