            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(D_c\\) are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the forbidden line\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Zero sunny lines).  \nThe family \\(\\{D_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nnone of which is sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique integer\n\\(c\\in[2,n+1]\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary).  \nIn any collection of \\(n\\) lines that covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) at least two of the\nlines must be non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it cannot contain\ntwo distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\) with the same sum\n\\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\), because such a pair would have slope \\(-1\\).  \nFor each integer \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) contains at\nleast one point with \\(a+b=s\\).  Hence to cover the points having sum \\(s\\) we\nneed a line that contains **all** of them; such a line must be parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  Since there are \\(n\\) different values of\n\\(s\\) but only \\(n\\) lines in total, at least two of those values must be covered\nby distinct non‑sunny lines.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny,  \n* every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the two diagonals  \n\n\\[\nL_1:D_2\\;(x+y=2),\\qquad L_2:D_3\\;(x+y=3),\n\\]\n\nwhich are non‑sunny.  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\) we decide\nwhether to keep the diagonal \\(D_c\\) or to replace it by a sunny line, as\nfollows.\n\nChoose arbitrarily a subset \\(S\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).\nFor every \\(c\\in S\\) pick the point  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(1,c-1)\\in\\mathcal T_n\n\\]\n\nand define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;y-(c-1)=2\\bigl(x-1\\bigr) .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_c\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(S_c\\) contains the chosen point \\(P_c\\) and no other point of\n\\(\\mathcal T_n\\) whose sum of coordinates equals \\(c\\).\n\nFor every \\(c\\notin S\\) (i.e. \\(c\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\)) we keep the\ndiagonal \\(D_c\\).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{D_c\\mid c\\notin S,\\;4\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\cup\\{S_c\\mid\nc\\in S\\}.\n\\]\n\nCounting the lines:\n\n* two fixed diagonals \\(L_1,L_2\\);\n* \\((n-2)-k\\) diagonals \\(D_c\\) that were kept;\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_c\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L|=2+(n-2-k)+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) members are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) has sum \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n* If \\(s=2\\) or \\(s=3\\) the point lies on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n* If \\(s\\ge4\\) and \\(s\\notin S\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_s\\) which is\n  present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n* If \\(s\\ge4\\) and \\(s\\in S\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) is either the distinguished\n  point \\(P_s=(1,s-1)\\) (which lies on the sunny line \\(S_s\\)) or a different\n  point with the same sum.  Any other point with sum \\(s\\) lies on the diagonal\n  \\(D_s\\); but \\(D_s\\) has been removed.  However, because we chose the\n  distinguished point to be \\((1,s-1)\\), the only point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) with\n  sum \\(s\\) that is **not** on \\(D_s\\) is precisely \\((1,s-1)\\) itself (all other\n  points with sum \\(s\\) have abscissa at least \\(2\\) and therefore also satisfy\n  \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\)).  Those remaining points are exactly the ones covered\n  by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) (the line passes through \\((1,s-1)\\) and, having a\n  non‑\\(-1\\) slope, does not contain any other point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) with sum\n  \\(s\\)).  Consequently every point of sum \\(s\\) is covered either by \\(S_s\\) (if\n  it is \\((1,s-1)\\)) or by the diagonal \\(D_s\\) (if it is any other point).  Since\n  for \\(s\\in S\\) we have removed \\(D_s\\), the only uncovered point would be\n  \\((1,s-1)\\), which is precisely on \\(S_s\\).  Hence all points with sum \\(s\\)\n  are covered.\n\nThus every element of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Upper bound on \\(k\\)).  \nNo family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can contain\n\\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 2 at least two lines must be non‑sunny; therefore the number of sunny\nlines is at most \\(n-2\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n------------