t works for the other two forbidden directions. ∎  \n\n   Because the three forbidden directions are mutually distinct, the total number of non‑sunny lines is at most \\(2\\cdot 3=6\\). However we need a bound that depends on \\(n\\).  \n   Consider the \\(n\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\). Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies in exactly one column. A sunny line is not vertical, hence it meets each column in **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\). Consequently a sunny line can “replace’’ at most one vertical line in a covering that originally used all \\(n\\) vertical lines. Starting from the covering by the \\(n\\) vertical lines, each time we substitute a vertical line by a sunny line we lose the ability to cover the points of that column that are not on the sunny line. Since each column contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\) (except possibly the last column, which contains exactly one point), we can perform this substitution for at most \\(n-2\\) columns without leaving any point uncovered. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 2.* For any integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - Begin with the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n     \\]\n\n     They cover \\(S_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n\n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) replace the vertical line \\(V_i\\) by the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n     By construction \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n     \\[\n     P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in S_n .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n   - The remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) are kept unchanged.\n\n   We now verify that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the obtained lines.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \n   The vertical line \\(V_a\\) is still present, so \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \n   If \\(b=n+1-a\\) then \\((a,b)=P_a\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n   Otherwise \\(b\\le n-a\\). Since \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\ge 1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\ge k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if \\(b\\le k\\) then \\(a+b\\le k+k\\le 2k\\le 2(n-2)<n+1\\), which would force \\(b\\ge n+1-a\\), contradicting the assumption \\(b\\neq n+1-a\\). Hence \\(b\\ge k+1\\). Consequently the vertical line \\(V_b\\) (which has not been removed) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   In all cases the point is covered. The lines are distinct because the slopes of the \\(L_i\\) are \\(\\frac12\\) while the \\(V_j\\) are vertical, and the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-intercepts. Thus we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
