"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. any non\u2011negative integer not larger than \\(n-2\\) can occur, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The set \\(S_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points; it is the triangular region that must be covered.\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\), at most \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - Call a line **vertical** if it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, **horizontal** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, and **diagonal** if it is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   - Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies in exactly one of the \\(n\\) vertical strips \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   - A sunny line is **not** vertical, hence it meets each vertical strip in at most one point of \\(S_n\\). (If a sunny line met a strip in two distinct points of \\(S_n\\), those two points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, a contradiction.)  \n\n   Consequently, if we delete a vertical line from a covering, the points that were covered only by that vertical line must be covered by sunny lines, and each sunny line can supply at most one of those points.  \n\n   In the triangular set \\(S_n\\) the first and the last vertical strips contain only one point each (the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\)), while every other strip contains at least two points. Hence, to replace a vertical line by sunny lines we need at least as many sunny lines as the number of points that were the *only* points of that strip. Because only the two extreme strip