lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\n     *Case 2: \\(a\\in R\\).*  \n     Write \\(a=i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).  \n     If \\(b=i-1\\) then \\((a,b)=P_i\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n     Otherwise \\(b\\neq i-1\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     a+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\le n+1-i.\n     \\]\n\n     Since \\(i\\ge 2\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(n+1-i\\ge k+1\\). Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\ge k+1.\n     \\]\n\n     Consequently the vertical line \\(V_b\\) has **not** been removed (the removed columns are exactly the numbers \\(2,\\dots ,k+1\\)), so \\(V_b\\) belongs to the family and contains the point \\((a,b)\\) because its equation is \\(x=b\\).  \n\n     Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n   - **Step 5 \u2013 distinctness of the lines.**  \n     All vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, and each sunny line has slope \\(\\frac12\\) whereas the vertical lines have undefined slope; therefore no sunny line coincides with a vertical line, and the sunny lines are distinct from each other because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts. Hence the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   The construction contains exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\mid i\\in R\\}\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family exists. Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e"
