            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. any non‑negative integer not larger than \\(n-2\\) can occur, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The set \\(S_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points; it is the right‑angled triangular region that must be covered.\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\), at most \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny.  \n   The proof uses the fact that a sunny line can meet a fixed vertical line in at most one point of \\(S_n\\); consequently, if a vertical line \\(x=i\\) is omitted, at least \\(|C_i|\\) sunny lines are needed to replace it, where \\(C_i\\) is the column \\(\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\). Since \\(|C_i|\\ge 2\\) for every non‑extreme column, at most the two extreme vertical lines can be omitted, giving the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   *Lemma 2.* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n   The construction is split into two sub‑cases.\n\n   *Case A – \\(k\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).*  \n   Keep the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1)\\ \\text{and}\\ j=n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  \n   For the \\(k\\) columns whose vertical lines are omitted,\n   \\[\n   R=\\{\\,n-k,\\;n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\n   \\]\n   introduce the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = (n+1-i)+\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad(i\\in R).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and (2) passes through the top point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) of the removed column.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a\\notin R\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (1) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   - If \\(a\\in R\\) write \\(a=i\\).  \n     *If \\(b=n+1-i\\)*, the point is on \\(L_i\\).  \n     *If \\(b\\neq n+1-i\\)*, then \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i-1\\le k+1\\) (because \\(i\\ge n-k\\)).  \n     Since \\(k\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) we have \\(k+1\\le n-k-1\\); consequently \\(b\\notin R\\) and the vertical line \\(V_b\\) (which we kept) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line.  \n   The family consists of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines, all distinct, and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Case B – \\(\\displaystyle k>\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).*  \n   Apply the construction of Case A to the *transpose* of the problem, i.e. exchange the roles of the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-(n-1-k)-1)\\ \\text{and}\\ j=n ,\n   \\]\n   and for the omitted rows introduce sunny lines of slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) that pass through the rightmost point of each omitted row.  \n   Because the transpose exchanges columns and rows, the same argument as in Case A shows that all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and that exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines appear.  \n\n   Since \\(k\\le n-2\\), the number of omitted rows is \\(n-1-k\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\), so the construction of Case A can indeed be applied after transposition.\n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Lemma 2 shows that this condition is also sufficient: for each admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n   Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The statement holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
