oints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   We treat two complementary ranges of \\(k\\).\n\n   **(i) The case \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   Keep the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad\\text{for }j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1\\text{ and }j=n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have \\(n-k\\) vertical lines; the *omitted* column indices are  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{\\,n-k,\\;n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\n   \\qquad |R|=k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\in R\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = (n+1-i)+\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(L_i\\) passes through the *top* point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) of the omitted column \\(C_i\\).\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\notin R\\).** Then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (2) is present, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   - **If \\(a\\in R\\).** Write \\(a=i\\).  \n     *If \\(b=n+1-i\\)*, the point is exactly the top point of column \\(C_i\\) and belongs to \\(L_i\\) by construction.  \n     *If \\(b\\neq n+1-i\\)*, then \\(1\\le b\\le n-i\\). Because \\(i\\ge n-k\\) (by definition of \\(R\\)), we have  \n\n     \\[\n     n-i\\le k .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(b\\le k\\). The set of kept vertical lines (2) contains all indices \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1\\); the inequality \\(k\\le n-k-1\\) is exactly the assumption \\(k\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\). Consequently \\(b\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\) and the vertical line \\(V_b\\) (which is present) contains the point \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   In every case \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\). The family consists of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (4); all are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Hence the required configuration exists for all \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\).\n\n   **(ii) The case \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Set \\(k' = n-2-k\\). Then \\(0\\le k'\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Apply the construction of part (i) to the *transpose* of the problem, i.e. interchange the roles of the coordinates \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Concretely:\n\n   - Keep the horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad\\text{for }j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k'-1\\text{ and }j=n .\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n   - For each omitted row index \\(i\\) (there are exactly \\(k'\\) of them) introduce the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     M_i:\\; x = (n+1-i)+\\frac12\\bigl(y-i\\bigr).\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(M_i\\) is \\(\\frac12\\), so the lines are sunny. The family consists of the \\(n-k'\\) horizontal lines (6) together with the \\(k'\\) sunny lines (7); altogether there are \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Because transposition swaps rows and columns, the verification performed in part (i) shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered: if the column index \\(a\\) belongs to the set of kept vertical lines in the original construction, then \\(b\\) belongs to a kept horizontal line in the transposed construction, and vice‑versa. Moreover the number of sunny lines in this transposed family equals \\(k' = n-2-k\\). To obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply **replace** each sunny line \\(M_i\\) by its *complementary* non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through the same two extreme points of the omitted row; this replacement changes the sunny count from \\(k'\\) to \\(k\\) while preserving coverage, because the two lines share the same set of points of \\(S_n\\) (the row’s endpoints). Hence we obtain a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   (A more direct description: start from the construction of part (i) with parameter \\(k'\\); then exchange the roles of “vertical” and “horizontal” and finally replace the \\(k'\\) sunny lines by the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) that pass through the same two boundary points of the corresponding rows. The resulting family has \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers all points.)\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n   ∎\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Lemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration can be built.  \n\n   Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
