art (i) works provided the above gap is filled.\n\n##### Part (ii): \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor<k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Step:** Define \\(k' = n-2-k\\) and note \\(0\\le k'\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\). Apply the construction of part (i) after swapping \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). This yields a family of horizontal lines (6) and sunny lines (7) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Justification:* The transposition symmetry of the set \\(S_n\\) (i.e., \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) iff \\((b,a)\\in S_n\\)) makes this plausible. No gap here.\n\n**Step:** \u201cTo obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply replace each sunny line \\(M_i\\) by its complementary non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through the same two extreme points of the omitted row; this replacement changes the sunny count from \\(k'\\) to \\(k\\) while preserving coverage, because the two lines share the same set of points of \\(S_n\\) (the row\u2019s endpoints).\u201d\n\n*Issue (Justification Gap):*  \n- It is not shown that a line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through the two \u201cextreme points\u201d of a row indeed covers **all** points of that row that were previously covered by the sunny line \\(M_i\\). The sunny line \\(M_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac12\\) and passes through the top point of the omitted row; the slope \\(-1\\) line passes through the two endpoints \\((i, n+1-i)\\) and \\((n+1-i, i)\\). These two points are in \\(S_n\\), but the intermediate points of the row (with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate) are not on the slope \\(-1\\) line. Hence the claim that the set of points of \\(S_n\\) covered is unchanged is false. Consequently the replacement may leave some points uncovered, invalidating the construction.\n\nBecause this step is essential for achieving the required number of sunny lines in the range \\(k>\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\), the construction is incomplete.\n\n#### 5. Conclusion\nThe final statement that the possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,\\do