hose three points must be collinear with a rational slope $p/q$ in lowest terms, $|p|,|q|\\le n$. The only possibilities that give more than two points inside the triangle are slopes $1$ and $-2$ (and their reciprocals), each of which can contain at most $\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor$ points. A direct inspection shows that no sunny line can contain more than $\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor$ points, and for each $t$ in the range $1\\le t\\le\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor$ one can exhibit a sunny line with exactly $t$ points (e.g. the line $y=\\frac{t-1}{t}x+1$).\n\n3. **Deriving a necessary inequality.**  \n   Let $k$ be the number of sunny lines and $n-k$ the number of non‑sunny lines. Using the maximal possible numbers of points each type of line can cover we obtain inequality (1). Rearranging gives the lower bound (2) on $k$.\n\n4. **Partial conclusion.**  \n   The inequality shows that any admissible configuration must have at least $\\lceil n/2\\rceil$ sunny lines. The construction of examples achieving the lower bound, or any further restriction on the maximal possible $k$, remains open.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is horizontal ($y=$const), vertical ($x=$const) or has slope $-1$ (i.e. $x+y=$const). Otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points on a non‑sunny line)\n\n*Horizontal.*  \nFor $b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ the line $y=b$ meets $\\mathcal{P}_n$ in the points\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\},\n\\]\nhence in exactly $n+1-b\\le n$ points. The line $y=1$ contains $n$ points.\n\n*Vertical.*  \nAnalogously, $x=a$ meets $\\mathcal{P}_n$ in $n+1-a\\le n$ points, and $x=1$ contains $n$ points.\n\n*Slope $-1$.*  \nFor $c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}$ the line $x+y=c$ meets $\\mathcal{P}_n$ in the points\n\\[\n\\{(a