            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and no smaller \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Counting points.**  \n   The set of required lattice points is  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   *Horizontal* line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points; the line \\(y=1\\) attains the bound \\(n\\).  \n   *Vertical* line \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in \\(n+1-a\\le n\\) points; the line \\(x=1\\) also contains \\(n\\) points.  \n   *Slope \\(-1\\)* line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in \\(c-1\\le n\\) points; the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains \\(n\\) points.  \n   Hence **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)**, and the bound \\(n\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Let a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   If it contains three lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) then its slope is rational, say \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms. The integer points on the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\); inside the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the length of such a progression is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+|q|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Because \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), whence the maximal possible length is obtained for \\(|p|+|q|=2\\). This gives the universal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   for the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that a sunny line can contain.  \n   For every integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le M\\) a sunny line with exactly \\(t\\) points exists; for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\;y=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x+1\n   \\]\n\n   passes through the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(t,t)\\), all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(2t\\le n+1\\) when \\(t\\le M\\).\n\n4. **Necessary inequality.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), of which \\(k\\) are sunny and \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \n   Using the maximal capacities from steps 2 and 3 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   N\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N=n(n+1)/2\\) and \\(M=\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) and solving for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **any feasible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines**.\n\n5. **Sufficiency – construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Step A – choose the non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(s=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le s\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n   Take the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(x=i\\) contains the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); altogether the \\(s\\) vertical lines cover  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-i)=sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). (If \\(s=0\\) this sum is empty.)\n\n   *Step B – count the points still uncovered.*  \n   The total number of points is \\(N=n(n+1)/2\\). After removing the points covered in (4) the remainder is  \n\n   \\[\n   R=N-\\Bigl(sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\\Bigr)\n     =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-sn+\\frac{s(s-1)}2\n     =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Observe that \\(R\\) is exactly the number of points of the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n-s+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step C – cover the remaining triangle with sunny lines.*  \n   The triangle \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\\) has side length \\(n-s\\).  \n   Its points can be covered by the following \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\;y=\\frac{j-1}{j}\\,x+1\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   As noted in step 3, \\(\\ell_j\\) contains exactly \\(j\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\\); the sets of points contributed by different \\(\\ell_j\\) are disjoint because each \\(\\ell_j\\) contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(j,j)\\) and no point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) lies on any \\(\\ell_j\\).  \n   Hence the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2=R,\n   \\]\n\n   exactly the amount needed in (5).  \n\n   *Step D – distinctness of the lines.*  \n   The \\(s\\) vertical lines are distinct from each other and from the sunny lines because the latter have non‑vertical slopes. Moreover the sunny lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise distinct (different slopes). Thus we have produced \\(s+(n-s)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k=n-s\\) are sunny, covering every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Since \\(s=n-k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(\\lfloor n