            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and no smaller \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **The set of required points.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Capacity of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points; the bound \\(n\\) is attained by the lines \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence any non‑sunny line can cover **at most \\(n\\) points** of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Capacity of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   If the line contains two lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) then its slope is rational, say \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0\\)).  \n   All lattice points on the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the length of such a progression cannot exceed  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, moving one step changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) therefore limits the number of steps.)  \n   Because the line is sunny we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\); the right‑hand side of (1) is maximal when \\(|p|+|q|=2\\). Consequently every sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Moreover, for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le M\\) there exists a sunny line containing exactly \\(t\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   Take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y = x + c_t ,\\qquad c_t:=1-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Its integer points are \\((a,a+c_t)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(2a+c_t\\le n+1\\); the admissible values of \\(a\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,t\\). Hence \\(\\ell_t\\) contains precisely the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,(t,t),\n   \\]\n\n   all belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(2t\\le n+1\\) when \\(t\\le M\\). The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(1\\), which is not \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n\n4. **Necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), of which \\(k\\) are sunny and \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \n   Using the maximal capacities from (2) and from step 2 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   N\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N=n(n+1)/2\\) and \\(M=\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) and solving (3) for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **any feasible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines**.\n\n5. **Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be such a number and put \\(s:=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le s\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n\n   *Step A – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Use the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(x=i\\) contains the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); altogether the lines (5) cover  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-i)=sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Step B – the remaining triangle.*  \n   Removing the points covered in (6) leaves  \n\n   \\[\n   R=N-\\Bigl(sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\\Bigr)\n     =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n-s+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right‑isosceles triangle of side length \\(n-s\\).\n\n   *Step C – sunny lines covering the smaller triangle.*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k=n-s\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y = x + (1-j) .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   As shown in step 3, \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny and contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,(j,j),\n   \\]\n\n   all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\\) because \\(2j\\le n-s+1\\).  \n   Different values of \\(j\\) give disjoint point sets, since \\(\\ell_j\\) contains only points with equal coordinates and the maximal equal coordinate on \\(\\ell_j\\) is \\(j\\).  \n\n   Consequently the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2=R ,\n   \\]\n\n   exactly the number required in (7). Hence the lines (8) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\\).\n\n   *Step D – distinctness.*  \n   The vertical lines (5) are pairwise distinct and none of them is vertical‑slope‑\\(1\\); the sunny lines (8) have slope \\(1\\) and distinct intercepts, so all \\(s+(n-s)=n\\) lines are distinct. 