\]\n\n   The set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n-s+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right‑isosceles triangle of side length \\(n-s\\).\n\n   *Step C – sunny lines covering the smaller triangle.*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k=n-s\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y = x + (1-j) .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   As shown in step 3, \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny and contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,(j,j),\n   \\]\n\n   all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\\) because \\(2j\\le n-s+1\\).  \n   Different values of \\(j\\) give disjoint point sets, since \\(\\ell_j\\) contains only points with equal coordinates and the maximal equal coordinate on \\(\\ell_j\\) is \\(j\\).  \n\n   Consequently the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2=R ,\n   \\]\n\n   exactly the number required in (7). Hence the lines (8) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-s}\\).\n\n   *Step D – distinctness.*  \n   The vertical lines (5) are pairwise distinct and none of them is vertical‑slope‑\\(1\\); the sunny lines (8) have slope \\(1\\) and distinct intercepts, so all \\(s+(n-s)=n\\) lines are distinct. Among them precisely the \\(k=n-s\\) lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are sunny.\n\n   Because \\(s\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), the construction works for every \\(k\\) satisfying (4).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (4) and the explicit construction for all admissible \\(k\\) together give the exact set of possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and 