            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N:=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Capacity of non‑sunny lines  \n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points; the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\neach contain exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence any non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Capacity of sunny lines  \n\nConsider a sunny line \\(L\\) with equation \\(y=mx+b\\) where \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) contains two lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0\\)).  \nAll lattice points of \\(L\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+qt,\\;b_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is one lattice point on \\(L\\).  \nMoving from one lattice point to the next changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\).  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the number of lattice points of \\(L\\) that lie in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is sunny, \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\); the right–hand side of (1) is therefore bounded by  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **every sunny line contains at most \\(M\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), of which \\(k\\) are sunny and \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \nUsing the maximal capacities derived above we obtain  \n\n\\[\nN\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(N=n(n+1)/2\\) and \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nA short case distinction on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that (3) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad\\bigl(0\\le s\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(x=i\\) contains the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); consequently the lines (5) cover  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{vert}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-i)=sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The remaining points  \n\nAfter removing the points covered in (6) the number of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nR=N-C_{\\text{vert}}\n   =\\frac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-s,b)\\) we obtain the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a',b')\\mid a'\\ge1,\\;b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=\\mathcal P_{\\,k},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is a right‑isosceles triangle of side length \\(k\\) containing exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = 2x + (1-2j) .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\nA lattice point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\) iff \\(y=2x+1-2j\\).  \nWriting \\(x=j+t\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)) gives the parametrisation  \n\n\\[\n(j+t,\\;1+2t),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe conditions defining \\(\\mathcal Q\\) become  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nj+t\\ge s+1,\\\\[2pt]\n1+2t\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n(j+t)+(1+2t)\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(n=s+k\\) these inequalities are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n0\\le t\\le k-j .\n\\]\n\nHence the points of \\(\\ell_j\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal Q\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(j,1),\\;(j+1,3),\\;(j+2,5),\\dots ,\\bigl(j+(k-j),\\,2(k-j)+1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\ni.e. **\\(k-j+1\\) points**.  \nFor different values of \\(j\\) these point sets are disjoint, because the first coordinate of a point on \\(\\ell_j\\) lies in the interval \\([j,\\,k]\\) while on \\(\\ell_{j'}\\) it lies in \\([j',\\,k]\\) and the intervals are disjoint when \\(j\\neq j'\\).\n\nSumming over all \\(j\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}(k-j+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=R,\n\\]\n\nso the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Distinctness of the whole family  \n\nThe vertical lines (5) are pairwise distinct and none of them has slope \\(2\\).  \nThe sunny lines (8) all have slope \\(2\\) but distinct intercepts \\(1-2j\\); consequently they are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the vertical lines.  \nThus we have produced exactly \\(s+k=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\n- Inequality (4) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n- The construction of Sections 4.1–4.4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nTherefore the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\;\\dots ,\\;n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self‑Correction Note**  \nThe earlier (incorrect) attempt used only lines of slope 1 to cover the remaining triangle, which left many points uncovered. The present construction replaces those lines by the family \\(\\ell_j\\) of slope 2; each \\(\\ell_j\\) covers a whole “diagonal” of the triangle, and together they partition the triangle, guaranteeing full coverage. All counting arguments explicitly take possible overlaps into account, and the integrality of all coordinates is verified by the parametrisation (9).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
