e \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{\\text{vert}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-i)=sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   After removing the points covered in (4) the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-s,b)\\) shows that \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is exactly the set \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,k}\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal Q|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y = 2x + (1-2j) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   **Claim.**  For a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(\\ell_j\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal Q\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (j,1),\\;(j+1,3),\\;(j+2,5),\\dots ,\\bigl(k,\\,2(k-j)+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. precisely \\(k-j+1\\) points.\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   A lattice point on \\(\\ell_j\\) can be written as  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(j+t,\\;1+2t),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The conditions defining \\(\\mathcal Q\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   j+t\\ge s+1,\\\\[2pt]\n   1+2t\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   (j+t)+(1+2t)\\le n+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(n=s+k\\) these become\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   t\\ge s+1-j,\\\\[2pt]\n   t\\ge 0,\\\\[2pt]\n   3t\\le s+k-j .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(j\\le k\\) we have \\(s+1-j\\le k-j\\). Moreover \\(s+k-j = n-j\\) and \\(n-j\\ge 3(k-j)\\) (the inequality follows from \\(s=n-k\\le k\\), i.e. \\(n\\le 2k\\), which holds because \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)). Hence the three inequalities are equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le t\\le k-j .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting