(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines\n\nLet a sunny line be \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf the line contains two lattice points then \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0\\)).  \nAll lattice points on the line are  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+qt,\\;b_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p|+|q|\\).  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the number of lattice points of the line that lie in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\); consequently the maximal possible number of points is  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary lower bound for sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), of which \\(k\\) are sunny and \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \nUsing the capacities from Sections 1 and 2 we obtain\n\n\\[\nN\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(N=n(n+1)/2\\) and \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nA short case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that (2) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad(0\\le s\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor).\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(x=i\\) contains the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); altogether they cover  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{vert}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-i)=sn-\\frac{s(s-1)}2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The uncovered set  \n\nAfter removing the points covered in (5) the remaining set is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-s,b)\\) identifies \\(\\mathcal Q\\) with \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,k}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal Q|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = 2x + (1-2j) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); thus each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\n*Lemma.*  For a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(\\ell_j\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal Q\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(j,1),\\;(j+1,3),\\;(j+2,5),\\dots ,\\bigl(k,\\,2(k-j)+1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nhence precisely \\(k-j+1\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lattice points on \\(\\ell_j\\) are given by  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(j+t,\\;1+2t),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe three defining inequalities of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) become\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nj+t\\ge s+1,\\\\[2pt]\n1+2t\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n(j+t)+(1+2t)\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n=s+k\\) these are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nt\\ge s+1-j,\\\\[2pt]\nt\\ge 0,\\\\[2pt]\n3t\\le s+k-j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(j\\le k\\) we have \\(s+1-j\\le k-j\\).  \nMoreover \\(s+k-j=n-j\\ge 3(k-j)\\) (the inequality follows from \\(s=n-k\\le k\\), i.e. \\(n\\le 2k\\), which holds because \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)).  \nThus (9) reduces to  \n\n\\[\n0\\le t\\le k-j .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the integer values \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-j\\) into (8) yields exactly the points listed in the statement of the lemma, and there are \\(k-j+1\\) of them. ∎\n\nThe intervals \\([j,k]\\) for different \\(j\\) are disjoint, so the point sets belonging to distinct \\(\\ell_j\\) are disjoint. Consequently the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}(k-j+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|\\mathcal Q|,\n\\]\n\nwhich equals the number of points that remained uncovered after Step 4.1. Hence the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\).\n\n#### 4.4  Distinctness of all lines  \n\nThe vertical lines (4) are pairwise distinct and have undefined slope, whereas the sunny lines (7) all have slope \\(2\\) but distinct intercepts \\(1-2j\\); therefore all \\(s+k=n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\n- Inequality (3) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n- The construction of Sections 4.1–4.4 demonstrates that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nHence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\;\\dots ,\\;n\\; } .\n\\]"
