s ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Projection Lemma)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \nThen for any integer \\(c\\) the vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one\npoint. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{\\,a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid \\exists\\,b\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell\\bigr\\}\\bigr|\\le 1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical it can be written as \\(y=mx+b\\) with a real slope\n\\(m\\). For a fixed \\(x=c\\) the equation yields a unique \\(y\\); hence at most one\nintersection point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Necessity of a vertical line for each abscissa)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\). Then for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)\nthere is a line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) that is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and has\nequation \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The set  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at least one point (namely \\((a,1)\\)). All points of \\(C_a\\) share the\nsame abscissa \\(a\\). Suppose that none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\). Then every line covering a point of \\(C_a\\) is not\nvertical; by Lemma 1 each such line can contain **at most one** point of\n\\(C_a\\). Since \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\ge 1\\), we would need at least \\(|C_a|\\) distinct\nlines to cover \\(C_a\\). Summing over all \\(a\\) gives a total of at least\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, contradicting the hyp