hen for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)\nthe vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and consider  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(C_a\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points (at least one because\n\\(a\\le n\\)). All points of \\(C_a\\) share the same abscissa \\(a\\).\n\nSuppose that \\(\\mathcal L\\) does **not** contain the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\nThen every point of \\(C_a\\) must be covered by a line that is not vertical.\nBy Lemma 1 each such line can contain at most one point of \\(C_a\\); therefore\n\\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) distinct lines are required to cover \\(C_a\\).\n\nIf this happens for any column, the total number of distinct lines needed is at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are available. Hence\nthe vertical line \\(x=a\\) must be present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) for every\n\\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma 2 the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct and together they cover every point of\n\\(T_n\\): for a given abscissa \\(a\\) the line \\(x=a\\) contains all admissible\npoints \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Since we already have \\(n\\) lines, no\nadditional line can be present.\n\nAll the lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; consequently\nnone of them is sunny. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\nConversely, the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) indeed satisfies the two required\nconditions, showing that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which such a