onclusion on sunny lines.**  \n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none of them is\n   sunny.  Hence the number of sunny lines is forced to be \\(k=0\\).  The\n   construction above shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and the argument in\n   steps\u202f3\u20134 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Projection Lemma)\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, then for each\ninteger \\(c\\) the vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point.\nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate equals \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line can be written uniquely as \\(y=mx+b\\) with real slope\n\\(m\\).  Substituting \\(x=c\\) yields the single value \\(y=mc+b\\); hence at most\none intersection point with the vertical line \\(x=c\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Every column needs its vertical line)\n\nFor each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(a\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(b\\le n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=n+1-a\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(C_a\\) share the same abscissa \\(a\\).  Suppose that\n\\(x=a\\notin\\mathcal L\\).  Then each point of \\(C_a\\) must be covered by a line\nthat is not vertical.  By Lemma\u202f1 a non\u2011