at most one point of each column \\(C_a\\),\nand there are exactly \\(n\\) columns (those with \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument\n\nAssume that a set \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Let \\(m\\) be the number of\nnon\u2011vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (\\(0\\le m\\le n\\)).  The remaining\n\\(n-m\\) lines are vertical.\n\n*Upper bound for the number of covered points.*  \nEach of the \\(m\\) non\u2011vertical lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points by\nLemma\u202f2, so together they cover at most \\(mn\\) points.  \nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) covers exactly the whole column \\(C_a\\), i.e.\n\\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) points.  Let \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of\ncolumn indices of the vertical lines; then \\(|S|=n-m\\) and the vertical\nlines cover  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in S}(n+1-a)= (n-m)n-\\sum_{a\\in S}a+(n-m) \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Adding the contributions we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{covered}\\le mn+(n-m)n-\\sum_{a\\in S}a+(n-m)\n               = n^{2}-\\sum_{a\\in S}a+(n-m). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Necessary condition.*  \nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (4) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\)\ngiven by (2):\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\sum_{a\\in S}a+(n-m)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (5) is minimized when the sum \\(\\sum_{a\\in S}a\\) is\nas small as possible, i.e. when \\(S\\) consists of the smallest \\(n-m\\)\nindices:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in S}a\\ge\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this lower bound into (5) yields\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2+(n-m)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nAfter elementary algebra,\n\n\\[\n(n-m)(n-m-1)\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n-m\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, the inequality forces  \n\n\\[\nn-m\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n-m=0\\) then \\(m=n\\) and all lines are non\u2011vertical.  In that case the\ntotal number of points that can be covered is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\),\nwhich is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every\n\\(n\\ge 3\\); hence this case is impossible.\n\nThus we must have \\(n-m=1\\); i.e. **exactly one vertical line** and\n\\(m=n-1\\) non\u2011vertical lines.\n\nLet the unique vertical line be \\(x=a_{0}\\) (\\(1\\le a_{0}\\le n\\)).\nIt covers \\(|C_{a_{0}}|=n+1-a_{0}\\) points.  Each of the remaining\n\\(n-1\\) non\u2011vertical lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points, so the total\nnumber of points that can be covered is at most\n\n\\[\n(n-1)n+(n+1-a_{0})=n^{2}+1-a_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n\n\\[\nn^{2}+1-a_{0}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}= \\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{n^{2}}2\\ge\n\\frac{3}{2}>1-a_{0}\\).  Hence even the case \\(n-m=1\\) cannot cover all\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nWe have reached a contradiction under the assumption that a non\u2011vertical\nline exists.  Therefore **every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be vertical**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction and Sunny\u2011line count\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence all required points are covered.\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, and a *sunny* line is defined\nas a line **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Consequently none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny, i.e.\nthe number of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe construction shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and the previous\nargument proves that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0