\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}|C_a|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Projection)\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, then for each integer \\(c\\) the vertical\nline \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) can\ncontain at most one point of any column \\(C_c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  A non‑vertical line has equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with a finite slope\n\\(m\\).  Substituting \\(x=c\\) yields the unique point \\((c,mc+b)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Horizontal projection)\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not horizontal, then for each integer \\(r\\) the\nhorizontal line \\(y=r\\) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\)\ncontains at most one point of any row \\(R_r\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Analogous to Lemma 1. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Maximum points on a slanted line)\n\nA line that is neither vertical nor horizontal can contain at most \\(n\\) points\nof \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma 1 it can meet at most one point of each of the \\(n\\) columns,\nso at most \\(n\\) points altogether. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main counting argument\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of\n\\(T_n\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\ns=n-v-h\n\\]\n\n(the number of slanted lines).  The slanted lines together cover at most\n\\(sn\\) points by Lemma 3.\n\nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) covers exactly the whole column \\(C_a\\); a horizontal\nline \\(y=b\\) covers exactly the whole row \\(R_b\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{a\\mid x=a\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad B=\\{b\\mid y=b\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(|A|=v,\\ |B|=h\\).  The points covered by the vertical and horizontal lines\namount to  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in A}(n+1-a)+\\sum_{b\\in B}(n+1-b)\n= vn-\\sum_{a\\in A}a+v+hn-\\sum_{b\\in B}b+h .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the slanted lines we obtain an upper bound for the\ntotal number of covered points:\n\n\\[\n\\text{covered}\\le sn+vn+hn-\\sum_{a\\in A}a-\\sum_{b\\in B}b+(v+h)\n= n^{2}-\\sum_{a\\in A}a-\\sum_{b\\in B}b+(v+h). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (2) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\)\ngiven by (A):\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\sum_{a\\in A}a-\\sum_{b\\in B}b+(v+h)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sums \\(\\sum_{a\\in A}a\\) and \\(\\sum_{b\\in B}b\\) are minimized when \\(A\\)\nand \\(B\\) consist of the smallest possible indices, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in A}a\\ge\\frac{v(v+1)}2,\\qquad\n\\sum_{b\\in B}b\\ge\\frac{h(h+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these minima into (3) yields the strongest necessary inequality:\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\frac{v(v+1)}2-\\frac{h(h+1)}2+(v+h)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4) gives\n\n\\[\n(v-1)(v-2)+(h-1)(h-2)\\le 0 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((x-1)(x-2)\\ge 0\\) for every integer \\(x\\) except \\(x=0,1,2\\), (5)\nforces  \n\n\\[\nv\\in\\{0,1,2\\},\\qquad h\\in\\{0,1,2\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(v\\ge 2\\) then \\((v-1)(v-2)>0\\); to keep the sum in (5) non‑positive we would\nneed \\((h-1)(h-2)<0\\), which is impossible because the same product is never\nnegative for integer \\(h\\).  Hence the only possibilities are\n\n\\[\n(v,h)=(0,0),\\;(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,1). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that each of these four cases is impossible unless\n\\(s=0\\) (i.e. there are **no** slanted lines).\n\n*Case \\((v,h)=(0,0)\\).*  All \\(n\\) lines are slanted.  Each can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points, so at most \\(n^{2}\\) points in total.  Equality would require\nevery line to meet every column, which would force all lines to be parallel,\ncontradicting distinctness.  Hence this case cannot cover all points of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case \\((v,h)=(1,0)\\) (or symmetrically \\((0,1)\\)).*  Suppose there is exactly\none vertical line \\(x=a_{0}\\).  It covers \\(|C_{a_{0}}|=n+1-a_{0}\\) points.\nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) slanted lines cover at most \\((n-1)n\\) points, so the\ntotal is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n+(n+1-a_{0})=n^{2}+1-a_{0}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}= \\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{n^{2}}2\\le -\\frac32\\).\nThus not all points can be covered.\n\n*Case \\((v,h)=(1,1)\\).*  Let the vertical line be \\(x=a_{0}\\) and the\nhorizontal line be \\(y=b_{0}\\).  Together they cover  \n\n\\[\n(n+1-a_{0})+(n+1-b_{0})-1 = 2n+1-a_{0}-b_{0}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points.  The remaining \\(n-2\\) slanted lines cover at most\n\\((n-2)n\\) points, giving a total at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)n+2n+1-a_{0}-b_{0}=n^{2}+1-a_{0}-b_{0}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nHence this case is also impossible.\n\nConsequently the only viable possibility is \\(s=0\\); i.e. **all** \\(n\\) lines are\nparallel to one of the three forbidden directions (vertical, horizontal, or\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  Such lines are not sunny by definition.  Therefore any admissible\nfamily contains **no** sunny line, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the\npoint \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\)), and contains no sunny line.  Hence\n\\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion** – The set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a\nconfiguration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]"
