"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a positive integer \\(n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of lattice points that have to be covered.\n\n2. **Base construction (no sunny lines).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_a:\\;x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   are not sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line.**  \n   Suppose a vertical line \\(x=a\\) is removed.  \n   All points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,1),\\,(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,n+1-a\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   They lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_a:\\;y=-(a-1)x+(a+n) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_a\\) equals \\(-(a-1)\\); for \\(a\\ge 2\\) this slope is\n   neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently \\(S_a\\) is a sunny line, and it contains **all**\n   points of the removed vertical column.\n\n4. **Inductive replacement.**  \n   Starting from the vertical family \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) we may replace\n   any subset of the vertical lines by the corresponding sunny lines\n   given by (1).  \n   If we replace exactly \\(k\\) vertical lines we obtain a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines containing all points of \\(T_n\\) and having\n   precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   For different values \\(a\\neq a'\\) the lines \\(S_a\\) and \\(S_{a'}\\) have\n   different slopes \\(-(a-1)\\neq -(a'-1)\\); therefore they are distinct.\n   Moreover no sunny line coincides with a remaining vertical line,\n   because a sunny line has finite slope while a vertical line is\n   parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   By choosing any subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of size \\(k\\) we obtain a\n   configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), all those\n   values are attainable.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny*, i.e. are not parallel to\n  the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(L_a\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering\ncondition with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line that replaces a given vertical line\n\nFix an integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) having first coordinate \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,n+1-a\\bigr).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points in (2) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nS_a:\\;y=-(a-1)x+(a+n) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, substituting \\(x=a\\) into the right\u2013hand side of (3) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=-(a-1)a+(a+n)= -a^2+a+a+n = n+1-a,\n\\]\n\nand the values \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\) are obtained by moving along the\nline while keeping \\(x=a\\).  The slope of \\(S_a\\) equals \\(-(a-1)\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) the slope is \\(0\\); the line \\(S_1\\) coincides with the\nhorizontal line \\(y=n\\) and is **not** sunny.  For \\(a\\ge 2\\) the slope\n\\(-(a-1)\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); consequently \\(S_a\\)\nis sunny.*\n\nThus, for every \\(a\\ge 2\\) the sunny line \\(S_a\\) contains **all**\npoints of the column \\(x=a\\) that belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nStart from the vertical family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (which gives\n\\(k=0\\)).  \nChoose any subset \\(A\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|A|=k\\).\nFor each \\(a\\in A\\) delete the vertical line \\(L_a\\) and insert the\nsunny line \\(S_a\\) defined in (3).  The resulting family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k=\n\\bigl\\{L_a\\mid a\\notin A\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{S_a\\mid a\\in A\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_a\\) with \\(a\\in A\\))\nand \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\notin A\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical\nline \\(L_a\\).  If \\(a\\in A\\) then, by construction, \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(S_a\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nFor distinct \\(a,a'\\) the slopes of \\(S_a\\) and \\(S_{a'}\\) are\n\\(-(a-1)\\) and \\(-(a'-1)\\); they are different, so the lines are distinct.\nA sunny line has finite slope, whereas each remaining vertical line is\nparallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; therefore no sunny line coincides with a\nvertical line.  Consequently \\(\\mathcal{F}_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.\n\nThus for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions\nand containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values\n\nThe definition of \\(k\\) already forces \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); the construction\nabove shows that every integer in this interval is attainable.  Hence\nno other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are possible, and no\nother values are.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"
