lopes are\ndifferent, so the lines \\(S_a\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose an arbitrary subset  \n\n\\[\nA\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |A|=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{L_a\\mid a\\notin A\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{S_a\\mid a\\in A\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  The family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines: we keep the\n\\(n-|A|=n-k\\) vertical lines whose indices are not in \\(A\\) and we add the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_a\\) for \\(a\\in A\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  By construction the only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) are\nthe \\(S_a\\) with \\(a\\in A\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) has precisely\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\notin A\\) then the vertical line \\(L_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal\n  F_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a\\in A\\) then \\((a,b)\\) is one of the points listed in (1), and\n  all those points lie on \\(S_a\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  For \\(a\\neq a'\\) the lines \\(S_a\\) and \\(S_{a'}\\) have\ndifferent slopes, so they are distinct.  A sunny line has a finite,\nnon‑zero slope, whereas each remaining vertical line is parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis; consequently no sunny line coincides with a vertical line.\nHence \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required\nproperties and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there are \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA sunny line is not vertical; consequently a sunny line can contain at\nmost one point from any fixed column \\(\\{x=a\\}\\).  Column \\(x=1\\) of\n\\(T_n\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n\\]\n\nCovering these \\(n\\) points would therefore require at least \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, and all of them would already be used for this column.  No line\nwould remain to cover any point with \\(x\\ge 2\\), contradicting the covering\ncondition.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]"
