            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(and therefore not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis) and also\ndifferent from \\(-1\\) (so it is not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\le n\\), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\nThus \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering requirement with\n\\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that replace vertical lines \\(x=a\\) for \\(a\\ge 2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,n+1-a\\bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points in (1) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nS_a:\\; y=-(a-1)x+(a+n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, substituting \\(x=a\\) into the right–hand side of (2) yields  \n\n\\[\ny