            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), from\n\\(\\infty\\) (i.e. the line is not vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (i.e. it is not\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal V=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nnone of which is sunny.  If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\le n\\), so the point\nlies on \\(V_a\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal V\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and\nrealises \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line that can replace two vertical lines\n\nFix an integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\) and define  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\; y=-(n-a)\\,x+(n+1). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-(n-a)\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore\n\\(L_a\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct computation shows that \\(L_a\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_a=(a,1),\\qquad Q_a=(a+1,n-a). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\ny_{P_a}=-(n-a)a+(n+1)=1,\\qquad\ny_{Q_a}=-(n-a)(a+1)+(n+1)=n-a .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Effect of inserting \\(L_a\\)\n\nReplace the two vertical lines \\(V_a\\) and \\(V_{a+1}\\) by the single sunny\nline \\(L_a\\).  Denote the resulting family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_a=\\bigl(\\mathcal V\\setminus\\{V_a,V_{a+1}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_a\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal F_a\\) contains \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and one sunny line, i.e. a\ntotal of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\n*Column \\(x=a\\).*  \nThe point \\((a,1)=P_a\\) is now on \\(L_a\\).  Any other point of this column has\ncoordinates \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); it remains on the still\npresent line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=a+1\\).*  \nThe point \\((a+1,n-a)=Q_a\\) is on \\(L_a\\).  All other points \\((a+1,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n-a-1\\) stay on \\(V_{a+1}\\).\n\n*All other columns.*  \nThe corresponding vertical lines are untouched, so every point in those\ncolumns is covered.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_a\\) satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly\none sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n2\\le a_1<a_2<\\dots <a_k\\le n-1\n\\]\n\nsuch that the intervals \\([a_i,a_i+1]\\) are pairwise disjoint (this is always\npossible because at most \\(n-2\\) disjoint pairs of consecutive columns exist).\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal V\\), perform the replacement of step 3 for each\nchosen \\(a_i\\).  After the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\Bigl(\\mathcal V\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{V_{a_i},V_{a_i+1}\\}\\Bigr)\n\\cup\\{L_{a_i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nWe removed \\(2k\\) vertical lines and added \\(k\\) sunny lines, so the total\nremains \\(n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(L_{a_i}\\) are sunny; therefore \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) has\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEach individual replacement preserves coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\); the\ndisjointness of the intervals guarantees that the replacements do not\ninterfere with one another.  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe vertical lines are distinct.  The slopes of the sunny lines are\n\\(-(n-a_i)\\), which are distinct because the \\(a_i\\) are distinct; hence the\nsunny lines are pairwise distinct and none coincides with a vertical line.\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\ncovering condition and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Let the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines be *non‑sunny*; by definition they are vertical, horizontal, or\nof slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Column 1.*  \nColumn 1 of \\(T_n\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).\nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it meets this column in at most one\npoint.  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) of the\n\\(n\\) points of column 1.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) points must be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  Since a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\)\ncontains at most one point of column 1, at least one of the non‑sunny lines\nmust be vertical, i.e. the line \\(x=1\\).  Thus **at least one** non‑sunny\nline is required.\n\n*First two columns.*  \nColumns 1 and 2 together contain  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)=2n-1\n\\]\n\npoints.  A sunny line can intersect each of these two columns in at most one\npoint, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) of the \\(2n-1\\) points.\nThe remaining \\(2n-1-2k\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\nlines.  A single vertical line lies in exactly one column, therefore a\nsingle non‑sunny line can contribute points from at most one of the two\ncolumns.  Consequently we need **at least two** non‑sunny lines to cover both\ncolumns completely.  Hence\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nAll integers  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2\n\\]\n\nare attainable, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Therefore the set\nof admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
